WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.406
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.406 --> 00:00:06.908
This workshop is for people who have already tried an agent and understand the basic idea

3
00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:08.127
of a skill.

4
00:00:08.127 --> 00:00:14.629
You do not need to be an artificial intelligence researcher, and you do not need to

5
00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:16.255
build a large application.

6
00:00:16.255 --> 00:00:22.757
We will move from asking one agent to make a change toward designing a reliable workflow

7
00:00:22.757 --> 00:00:24.789
that several agents can follow.

8
00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:28.040
Our practice application is an issue triage board.

9
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.510
Imagine a support team deciding which fictional issues deserve attention first.

10
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:39.012
This is familiar enough to explain without a long business briefing, but gives us useful boundaries

11
00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:41.450
for rules, presentation, documentation, and tests.

12
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:46.026
The goal is not to create as many agents as possible.

13
00:00:46.026 --> 00:00:52.391
The goal is to know when a specialist helps, what information to give it, which tools

14
00:00:52.391 --> 00:00:56.766
it should have, and how to prove its result is correct.

15
00:00:56.766 --> 00:01:01.938
We will also see why two conversations can still overwrite the same file.

16
00:01:01.938 --> 00:01:07.905
This is an original, narrated slide lesson, not a recording of a live Copilot session.

17
00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:11.485
The live workshop includes demonstrations and four hands-on labs.

18
00:01:11.485 --> 00:01:17.850
Pause the video whenever you want to inspect a code example or try a step yourself.

19
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.991
By the end, you should be able to make four practical decisions.

20
00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:26.902
First, decide where a repeated instruction belongs.

21
00:01:26.902 --> 00:01:33.557
A rule that every change must follow is different from a specialist role, and both are

22
00:01:33.557 --> 00:01:37.716
different from a procedure used only for a particular task.

23
00:01:37.716 --> 00:01:43.123
Second, write a delegated task that a subagent can understand on its own.

24
00:01:43.123 --> 00:01:49.777
It should know the goal, the relevant context, the files it may touch, and what result

25
00:01:49.777 --> 00:01:50.609
to return.

26
00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:53.936
A vague request to help is not enough.

27
00:01:53.936 --> 00:01:57.264
Third, choose a coordination strategy for one repository.

28
00:01:57.264 --> 00:01:59.343
Sometimes separate worktrees are appropriate.

29
00:01:59.343 --> 00:02:04.750
Sometimes the simpler and safer choice is several readers and only one writer.

30
00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:11.485
Sometimes independent files can be assigned to separate writers, provided the shared contract is already fixed.

31
00:02:11.485 --> 00:02:14.753
Fourth, check the result using the actual requirements.

32
00:02:14.753 --> 00:02:21.288
Passing a test suite matters, but it does not replace inspecting the user flow or checking

33
00:02:21.288 --> 00:02:24.964
that a priority rule behaves correctly at its boundary.

34
00:02:24.964 --> 00:02:29.865
You will leave with a runnable starter, reusable examples, and lab solutions.

35
00:02:29.865 --> 00:02:36.400
Treat those solutions as an answer key, not as files to copy before attempting the exercise.

36
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.556
The session has a hard three-hour boundary.

37
00:02:40.556 --> 00:02:47.540
The first hour includes explanation and demonstrations; it is not an hour of slides followed by

38
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.413
extra demonstrations.

39
00:02:48.413 --> 00:02:51.033
The remaining two hours are hands-on.

40
00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:54.088
Before the event, complete the attendee preflight.

41
00:02:54.088 --> 00:03:01.073
Install a supported Node version, check your Git and Copilot access, open the starter, and run

42
00:03:01.073 --> 00:03:02.383
its baseline checks.

43
00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:08.058
Downloading software and resolving organizational policy issues should happen before the clock starts.

44
00:03:08.058 --> 00:03:14.169
For the first fifteen minutes we establish the vocabulary and tour the two interfaces.

45
00:03:14.169 --> 00:03:21.154
The next twenty minutes show how to create a useful custom agent and a skill, then

46
00:03:21.154 --> 00:03:22.900
delegate a real task.

47
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:30.300
The final twenty-five minutes of the first hour concentrate on safe parallel work, integration, and review.

48
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.700
The four labs are timed at thirty, thirty-five, forty, and fifteen minutes.

49
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.750
Their short checkpoints and debriefs are inside those allocations.

50
00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.800
There is no hidden additional break or setup period.

51
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:51.000
If you need a break, take it during a lab work period and use the hint

52
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.800
ladder to catch up.

53
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.300
The facilitator guide identifies the live demonstrations and optional extensions.

54
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:04.500
Extensions are deliberately outside the three-hour core, so advanced participants can go deeper without making everyone

55
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.400
else late.

56
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.988
Think of the model as the engine that proposes useful next steps.

57
00:04:11.988 --> 00:04:15.647
The harness is the machinery around that engine.

58
00:04:15.647 --> 00:04:20.221
It supplies context, exposes tools, handles approvals, and organizes execution.

59
00:04:20.221 --> 00:04:26.166
Changing the model does not automatically change the harness or its configuration format.

60
00:04:26.166 --> 00:04:28.910
A custom agent is a definition.

61
00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:36.228
For example, a reviewer definition says what to review, which tools to use, and how to

62
00:04:36.228 --> 00:04:37.143
report findings.

63
00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:44.460
You can select that reviewer directly, or another agent can invoke it for a delegated task.

64
00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.406
A subagent is the delegated execution, not necessarily another file you must author.

65
00:04:50.406 --> 00:04:53.150
Built-in agents can be subagents too.

66
00:04:53.350 --> 00:05:00.107
Separate context means the specialist can investigate without filling the main conversation with all its intermediate

67
00:05:00.107 --> 00:05:00.530
material.

68
00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.909
A skill is closer to a reusable recipe.

69
00:05:03.909 --> 00:05:09.399
It may contain a checklist, a reference document, a script, or an example.

70
00:05:09.399 --> 00:05:14.467
The agent loads it when relevant or when you invoke it explicitly.

71
00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:20.380
Finally, a session is the ongoing unit of work with its conversation and workspace.

72
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:26.293
A top-level session, a custom agent definition, and a subagent call are not interchangeable.

73
00:05:26.293 --> 00:05:33.050
Keeping those concepts separate makes the user interfaces and the configuration examples much easier to understand.

74
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.481
Here is the most important warning in the workshop.

75
00:05:38.481 --> 00:05:42.020
Separate conversations are not proof of separate files.

76
00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:49.099
If two agents use the same checkout and both edit the same module, one can overwrite

77
00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:50.426
the other's change.

78
00:05:50.426 --> 00:05:55.292
Imagine two cooks with separate recipe cards but one cutting board.

79
00:05:55.292 --> 00:05:59.273
Their instructions are separate; their working surface is shared.

80
00:05:59.273 --> 00:06:02.812
Separate subagent context is like separate recipe cards.

81
00:06:02.812 --> 00:06:09.890
A worktree is more like giving each cook a separate preparation area, although they still share

82
00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.218
the kitchen's utilities.

83
00:06:11.218 --> 00:06:14.757
Git worktrees separate working directories and normally branches.

84
00:06:14.757 --> 00:06:20.950
They do not automatically restrict access to your computer, credentials, network, or external services.

85
00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:25.345
Sandboxing and tool permissions address different parts of that problem.

86
00:06:25.345 --> 00:06:28.702
There can also be shared resources beyond code.

87
00:06:28.702 --> 00:06:32.478
Two servers may try to use the same port.

88
00:06:32.478 --> 00:06:35.414
Tests may write the same report file.

89
00:06:35.414 --> 00:06:39.190
Concurrent Git commands in one checkout share its index.

90
00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:45.064
Different branches in the same folder are not a safe substitute for different worktrees.

91
00:06:45.064 --> 00:06:51.776
Throughout the labs, we will identify the actual workspace, the allowed write set, and the shared

92
00:06:51.776 --> 00:06:53.874
resources before allowing parallel implementation.

93
00:06:53.874 --> 00:06:57.650
When you cannot establish safe boundaries, serialize the writes.

94
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:06.178
These materials use live official GitHub and VS Code documentation checked on November seventh, twenty twenty-six,

95
00:07:06.178 --> 00:07:11.112
rather than treating a model's training data as a product reference.

96
00:07:11.112 --> 00:07:15.599
An important example is the October twenty-eighth VS Code release.

97
00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:22.776
It documents the Copilot harness as the default for new sessions in both the editor and

98
00:07:22.776 --> 00:07:23.674
Agents window.

99
00:07:23.674 --> 00:07:27.262
Earlier examples often assume the older Local harness.

100
00:07:27.262 --> 00:07:31.749
That difference affects tool names, handoff support, and agent configuration.

101
00:07:31.749 --> 00:07:35.786
We will label the harness for each runnable example.

102
00:07:35.786 --> 00:07:42.964
A target field helps the editor interpret a file, but it does not switch the session's

103
00:07:42.964 --> 00:07:43.861
actual harness.

104
00:07:43.861 --> 00:07:45.656
You must check both.

105
00:07:45.656 --> 00:07:47.450
Availability is not uniform.

106
00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:53.879
The Copilot app is documented for all Copilot plans, subject to organizational policy.

107
00:07:53.879 --> 00:07:56.274
App Projects are public preview.

108
00:07:56.274 --> 00:08:00.107
The customization marketplace and connectors are preview experiences.

109
00:08:00.107 --> 00:08:03.461
Computer use is experimental and requires permission.

110
00:08:03.461 --> 00:08:09.210
None of those optional capabilities is necessary to finish our core labs.

111
00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.564
Every product claim has a source identifier.

112
00:08:12.564 --> 00:08:16.397
The source ledger provides canonical links and caveats.

113
00:08:16.397 --> 00:08:23.105
Before teaching this workshop later, recheck the ledger and the versions attendees actually have.

114
00:08:23.105 --> 00:08:30.771
A verification date is an honest snapshot, not a promise that a fast-moving interface will never

115
00:08:30.771 --> 00:08:31.250
change.

116
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:37.475
The GitHub Copilot app is a desktop application built around agent-driven development.

117
00:08:37.475 --> 00:08:40.791
Its official documentation supports Windows, macOS, and Linux.

118
00:08:40.791 --> 00:08:47.424
Think of it as a place to direct work and review results, rather than only a

119
00:08:47.424 --> 00:08:48.253
code editor.

120
00:08:48.253 --> 00:08:54.886
A Project is useful when a goal continues over time and may require several sessions or

121
00:08:54.886 --> 00:08:55.301
repositories.

122
00:08:55.301 --> 00:08:56.959
Projects are public preview.

123
00:08:56.959 --> 00:09:01.934
A session is the more concrete unit that works on a task.

124
00:09:01.934 --> 00:09:06.494
For this workshop, one repository and one bounded feature are enough.

125
00:09:06.494 --> 00:09:11.883
When you start a session, choose where it runs and where it edits.

126
00:09:11.883 --> 00:09:15.200
A new worktree provides a separate working copy.

127
00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:20.054
Current checkout uses existing files and can therefore overlap other writers.

128
00:09:20.054 --> 00:09:23.438
Read the workspace selector rather than assuming isolation.

129
00:09:23.438 --> 00:09:26.823
The app offers Plan, Interactive, and Autopilot modes.

130
00:09:26.823 --> 00:09:30.631
Plan allows you to review the approach before implementation.

131
00:09:30.631 --> 00:09:32.323
Interactive supports collaborative steering.

132
00:09:32.323 --> 00:09:39.092
Autopilot is appropriate for sufficiently clear tasks, but it does not remove the need to review

133
00:09:39.092 --> 00:09:39.938
the result.

134
00:09:39.938 --> 00:09:42.900
Tool permission settings are a separate decision.

135
00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.977
A session can still need approval for a command or external action.

136
00:09:47.977 --> 00:09:54.746
Use the Changes view and the actual test output to decide whether the work is ready.

137
00:09:54.746 --> 00:09:59.400
The app's convenience does not make every completed agent response correct.

138
00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:06.293
In the live demonstration, open the issue triage repository in the Copilot app.

139
00:10:06.293 --> 00:10:12.532
Before sending a prompt, point out the environment, workspace, mode, model, and permission settings.

140
00:10:12.532 --> 00:10:19.217
These controls answer different questions, so do not collapse them into one idea of autonomy.

141
00:10:19.217 --> 00:10:26.347
Start with a planning request: explain how to add a triage rule, identify affected files, and

142
00:10:26.347 --> 00:10:28.575
return test cases without editing.

143
00:10:28.575 --> 00:10:35.705
The result should tell us where the work belongs and what evidence will prove it correct.

144
00:10:35.705 --> 00:10:37.487
Next, review that plan.

145
00:10:37.487 --> 00:10:43.281
If it proposes a new framework, a database, or external credentials, redirect it.

146
00:10:43.281 --> 00:10:46.400
Our application is intentionally small and offline.

147
00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.920
We want practice with agents, not infrastructure troubleshooting.

148
00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:53.239
Allow implementation only after the scope is clear.

149
00:10:53.239 --> 00:10:58.634
While the agent works, inspect whether its edits stay in the assigned files.

150
00:10:58.634 --> 00:11:04.028
If it expands the scope, steer the same session with a concrete correction.

151
00:11:04.028 --> 00:11:09.007
At the end, open the complete diff and run the documented checks.

152
00:11:09.007 --> 00:11:13.157
A response saying all done is not our acceptance criterion.

153
00:11:13.157 --> 00:11:19.381
We need the rule's expected output, tests of its edge cases, and no unrelated edits.

154
00:11:19.381 --> 00:11:26.021
If the desktop app is unavailable under your organization's policy, perform the same bounded workflow in

155
00:11:26.021 --> 00:11:26.850
VS Code.

156
00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:31.000
The safety pattern is portable even when the buttons differ.

157
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.965
In VS Code, the Agents window is an agent-first interface alongside the regular editor.

158
00:11:37.965 --> 00:11:41.373
The documented command is Chat: Open Agents window.

159
00:11:41.373 --> 00:11:45.208
The command-line alternative is code, space, double dash agents.

160
00:11:45.208 --> 00:11:49.469
The primary sidebar helps you organize sessions and open customizations.

161
00:11:49.469 --> 00:11:54.155
The central area is the conversation where you direct the task.

162
00:11:54.155 --> 00:11:58.842
The secondary sidebar exposes files, changes, search, and available validation tools.

163
00:11:58.842 --> 00:12:05.659
The schematic on this slide is an original orientation diagram, not a screenshot of a particular

164
00:12:05.659 --> 00:12:06.085
build.

165
00:12:06.085 --> 00:12:12.050
Use the live interface in the demonstration and explain the actual labels attendees see.

166
00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:19.033
When you create a session for a Git repository, Branch uses the selected current folder, while

167
00:12:19.033 --> 00:12:23.696
New Worktree starts from committed files in a separate working directory.

168
00:12:23.696 --> 00:12:30.478
A new worktree requires a repository with a commit, and it does not automatically contain uncommitted

169
00:12:30.478 --> 00:12:31.326
custom-agent files.

170
00:12:31.326 --> 00:12:37.261
Multiple panes are useful for monitoring, but only one is active at a time.

171
00:12:37.261 --> 00:12:43.196
Check the active session before giving feedback, starting a terminal, or opening a browser.

172
00:12:43.196 --> 00:12:48.283
The editor and Agents window can continue supported sessions without starting over.

173
00:12:48.283 --> 00:12:51.250
Remote and cloud execution have additional requirements.

174
00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:58.187
For our labs, local execution keeps the setup small and makes it easy to inspect exactly

175
00:12:58.187 --> 00:12:59.450
which files changed.

176
00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:06.787
A Markdown file can be discovered in two environments yet behave differently in each.

177
00:13:06.787 --> 00:13:09.930
This is why we label our examples.

178
00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:17.115
For the Copilot harness, the current VS Code file reference documents built-in tool aliases including read,

179
00:13:17.115 --> 00:13:19.361
search, edit, execute, and agent.

180
00:13:19.361 --> 00:13:22.055
Native delegation uses the task tool.

181
00:13:22.055 --> 00:13:27.893
The skills field can name enabled skills to load into an agent's context.

182
00:13:27.893 --> 00:13:32.384
For Local, delegation uses the VS Code run subagent tool.

183
00:13:32.384 --> 00:13:38.221
Local supports an agents list to limit which specialists a coordinator may invoke.

184
00:13:38.221 --> 00:13:40.467
Local also supports custom-agent handoffs.

185
00:13:40.467 --> 00:13:47.652
Do not copy the Local agents list or handoffs into a Copilot-targeted example and expect the

186
00:13:47.652 --> 00:13:48.550
same effect.

187
00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:52.531
Conversely, Local does not accept the Copilot skills field.

188
00:13:52.531 --> 00:13:56.732
Local skills are discovered or explicitly invoked using supported mechanisms.

189
00:13:56.732 --> 00:14:03.454
The target property tells the editor which schema to interpret: vscode for Local, or github-copilot for

190
00:14:03.454 --> 00:14:03.874
Copilot.

191
00:14:03.874 --> 00:14:09.335
It does not change the runtime you selected in the Session Target control.

192
00:14:09.335 --> 00:14:15.637
For every example, first select the intended harness, then check discovery and actual tool activity.

193
00:14:15.637 --> 00:14:22.358
Editor completion is useful, but it is not proof that the runtime has a particular tool.

194
00:14:22.358 --> 00:14:29.080
Our fallback is always a self-contained prompt and a sequential workflow, not pretending unsupported fields are

195
00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:29.500
enforced.

196
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:34.042
A strong task prompt is a compact contract.

197
00:14:34.042 --> 00:14:41.127
It says what success looks like and gives the agent enough information to find and verify

198
00:14:41.127 --> 00:14:42.455
the right change.

199
00:14:42.455 --> 00:14:45.998
Compare make triage smarter with a specific request.

200
00:14:45.998 --> 00:14:49.983
Add deterministic priority classification for the fictional issue fixtures.

201
00:14:49.983 --> 00:14:52.639
Preserve the existing issue input shape.

202
00:14:52.639 --> 00:14:54.411
Do not add dependencies.

203
00:14:54.411 --> 00:14:58.396
Limit implementation to the priority module and its tests.

204
00:14:58.396 --> 00:15:03.266
Include a boundary case and a case that must remain unchanged.

205
00:15:03.266 --> 00:15:09.023
The second version is more useful because it constrains both behavior and scope.

206
00:15:09.023 --> 00:15:16.107
The agent can still choose implementation details, but it does not have to invent your business

207
00:15:16.107 --> 00:15:16.550
requirements.

208
00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:21.162
For delegation, add the allowed actions and the result format.

209
00:15:21.162 --> 00:15:26.457
A research worker should return paths, existing patterns, and unknowns without editing.

210
00:15:26.457 --> 00:15:30.870
An implementation worker should return changed files and validation evidence.

211
00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:37.488
A reviewer should not claim to have executed tests when it has only reading tools.

212
00:15:37.488 --> 00:15:44.548
Include a stopping condition: stop after the assigned feature passes its checks, or report the specific

213
00:15:44.548 --> 00:15:47.637
blocker rather than expanding into unrelated work.

214
00:15:47.637 --> 00:15:50.725
This is context engineering in practical terms.

215
00:15:50.725 --> 00:15:57.785
You are selecting the information the agent needs, not pasting the entire repository or an unrelated

216
00:15:57.785 --> 00:15:59.550
conversation into every call.

217
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:08.452
Repository instructions hold durable conventions such as the test command, the supported runtime, and the rule

218
00:16:08.452 --> 00:16:10.781
that fixtures must be synthetic.

219
00:16:10.781 --> 00:16:15.438
Those should not be repeated differently in every specialist definition.

220
00:16:15.438 --> 00:16:21.027
The repository-wide Copilot file is dot github slash copilot instructions dot markdown.

221
00:16:21.027 --> 00:16:28.479
Path-specific instructions can live in the instructions directory with an apply-to pattern, subject to the surface's

222
00:16:28.479 --> 00:16:28.944
support.

223
00:16:28.944 --> 00:16:36.396
AGENTS dot markdown is another supported instruction format in some experiences; do not assume identical activation

224
00:16:36.396 --> 00:16:37.794
across all clients.

225
00:16:37.794 --> 00:16:41.985
For our small starter, keep the shared guidance short.

226
00:16:41.985 --> 00:16:49.437
Describe the application, the commands, the contract boundary, and the rule against adding dependencies or changing

227
00:16:49.437 --> 00:16:51.300
shared files without coordination.

228
00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:54.528
There is an important inheritance caveat.

229
00:16:54.528 --> 00:17:02.601
GitHub's current custom-agent documentation says repository instructions are not included by default for custom subagents in

230
00:17:02.601 --> 00:17:03.610
Copilot CLI.

231
00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:08.152
Therefore, our delegated briefs explicitly include the critical invariants.

232
00:17:08.152 --> 00:17:13.702
An opt-in or client-specific conversion should not be your only defense.

233
00:17:13.702 --> 00:17:18.243
Instructions guide behavior, but do not enforce file permissions.

234
00:17:18.243 --> 00:17:24.298
Saying do not edit a file is useful guidance, not a lock.

235
00:17:24.298 --> 00:17:29.344
Use tools, approvals, scope checks, and review alongside that guidance.

236
00:17:29.344 --> 00:17:37.418
In the first lab, you will author and verify these instructions rather than turning them into

237
00:17:37.418 --> 00:17:40.950
a long document that nobody can evaluate.

238
00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.612
Here is a small custom-agent definition.

239
00:17:44.612 --> 00:17:51.579
The frontmatter gives it a name, explains when it is useful, and selects reading and search

240
00:17:51.579 --> 00:17:52.015
tools.

241
00:17:52.015 --> 00:17:57.675
The Markdown body tells it what decisions to make and how to report.

242
00:17:57.675 --> 00:17:59.417
The restriction is deliberate.

243
00:17:59.417 --> 00:18:03.771
This reviewer does not need terminal execution, edits, or delegation.

244
00:18:03.771 --> 00:18:08.125
Removing those tools makes the role easier to reason about.

245
00:18:08.125 --> 00:18:14.221
Still, verify actual runtime activity, because tool restrictions can differ by harness and client.

246
00:18:14.221 --> 00:18:19.881
The agent should report actionable behavior defects and missing tests with file references.

247
00:18:19.881 --> 00:18:23.800
It should explain the impact and suggest a fix.

248
00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:29.894
If it finds nothing, it should say so and identify checks it could not perform.

249
00:18:29.894 --> 00:18:34.609
To verify it, select the intended harness and start a fresh chat.

250
00:18:34.609 --> 00:18:40.896
Attach one known source file and its tests, ask for a review, then inspect the findings

251
00:18:40.896 --> 00:18:42.075
against the code.

252
00:18:42.075 --> 00:18:45.611
Also inspect the tool calls and the working tree.

253
00:18:45.611 --> 00:18:51.112
A reviewer with read-only tools should not report that it ran the test suite.

254
00:18:51.112 --> 00:18:53.470
Discovery is only the first checkpoint.

255
00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:58.578
A visible agent that produces vague advice is not yet a useful agent.

256
00:18:58.578 --> 00:19:02.900
In the workshop repository, starter templates are separated from completed answers.

257
00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:09.300
You will adapt a role to a real recurring task, not just rename an expert persona.

258
00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:14.770
A skill is a directory, not only a Markdown paragraph.

259
00:19:14.770 --> 00:19:20.320
The required SKILL dot markdown file starts with a name and a description.

260
00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:25.871
The name uses lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens, and matches the parent directory.

261
00:19:25.871 --> 00:19:31.421
The description states both what the skill does and when to use it.

262
00:19:31.421 --> 00:19:33.556
Our example is test changes.

263
00:19:33.556 --> 00:19:40.387
Its procedure identifies the tests covering a change, runs the documented commands, and reports passed, failed,

264
00:19:40.387 --> 00:19:41.668
and not-run checks.

265
00:19:41.668 --> 00:19:48.500
A reference file can explain the output format, and a supporting script can perform a deterministic

266
00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:48.926
check.

267
00:19:48.926 --> 00:19:54.050
Keep paths relative to the skill file when referring to its resources.

268
00:19:54.250 --> 00:20:01.272
If you move only SKILL dot markdown and leave the script behind, you have not installed

269
00:20:01.272 --> 00:20:02.589
the complete skill.

270
00:20:02.589 --> 00:20:06.100
Automatic relevance selection is useful but not guaranteed.

271
00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:09.172
Test explicit invocation and automatic selection separately.

272
00:20:09.172 --> 00:20:16.194
If a skill is available as a slash command, invoke it on a small known task

273
00:20:16.194 --> 00:20:20.583
and inspect whether its instructions and resources were actually used.

274
00:20:20.583 --> 00:20:24.533
A skill does not grant permission to execute commands.

275
00:20:24.533 --> 00:20:28.922
The active agent still needs the relevant tools and approvals.

276
00:20:28.922 --> 00:20:31.556
Review every script before trusting it.

277
00:20:31.556 --> 00:20:38.578
This is the first step toward reliable agent composition: give the specialist a repeatable procedure rather

278
00:20:38.578 --> 00:20:41.650
than hoping it remembers your preferred process.

279
00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:50.222
Skills help manage context because the agent can discover a concise description before loading the full

280
00:20:50.222 --> 00:20:52.027
procedure and its resources.

281
00:20:52.027 --> 00:20:54.735
This is often called progressive disclosure.

282
00:20:54.735 --> 00:20:58.798
It avoids putting every possible checklist into every conversation.

283
00:20:58.798 --> 00:21:01.506
But you must evaluate the behavior.

284
00:21:01.506 --> 00:21:05.568
A skill can exist on disk without being discovered.

285
00:21:05.568 --> 00:21:08.728
It can be discovered without being selected.

286
00:21:08.728 --> 00:21:12.790
It can be selected without successfully executing its script.

287
00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:14.595
Use three small probes.

288
00:21:14.595 --> 00:21:20.915
A positive request clearly matches the skill, such as validate the changed priority rule.

289
00:21:20.915 --> 00:21:27.685
A negative request, such as explain the application layout, should not unnecessarily run that procedure.

290
00:21:27.685 --> 00:21:32.650
An ambiguous request helps you see whether the description needs refinement.

291
00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.356
Inspect the result and tool activity.

292
00:21:35.356 --> 00:21:38.280
Did it read the referenced report format?

293
00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:40.786
Did it run the expected command?

294
00:21:40.786 --> 00:21:44.963
Did it distinguish a skipped check from a passing one?

295
00:21:44.963 --> 00:21:47.469
A convincing-looking report is not proof.

296
00:21:47.469 --> 00:21:54.152
Review scripts and data access before enabling a skill, especially one acquired from a marketplace or

297
00:21:54.152 --> 00:21:54.987
another repository.

298
00:21:54.987 --> 00:21:57.075
Skills can include executable code.

299
00:21:57.075 --> 00:22:00.417
Avoid loading too many skills into a specialist.

300
00:22:00.417 --> 00:22:04.176
Give it the few procedures relevant to its role.

301
00:22:04.176 --> 00:22:10.859
If the role has only read tools, it can describe a verification plan, but it cannot

302
00:22:10.859 --> 00:22:14.200
truthfully claim to have run an execution-based skill.

303
00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:18.896
Now we combine a role and a recipe.

304
00:22:18.896 --> 00:22:25.688
In the Copilot harness, the current VS Code reference supports a skills array in the custom-agent

305
00:22:25.688 --> 00:22:26.113
frontmatter.

306
00:22:26.112 --> 00:22:30.357
It names enabled skills to load into that agent's context.

307
00:22:30.358 --> 00:22:37.150
For example, an implementer can have read, search, edit, and execute tools plus the test changes

308
00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:37.574
skill.

309
00:22:37.574 --> 00:22:44.366
Its body still specifies the allowed files, the behavior to implement, and the required handoff evidence.

310
00:22:44.366 --> 00:22:47.338
The skill supplies the repeatable validation procedure.

311
00:22:47.338 --> 00:22:51.158
In Local, do not paste the Copilot skills field.

312
00:22:51.158 --> 00:22:57.950
Use supported skill discovery or explicit invocation and tell the worker to follow the relevant procedure.

313
00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:03.482
The repository includes harness-labelled examples so you can compare them without guessing.

314
00:23:03.482 --> 00:23:10.592
Preloading a skill does not prove it ran, and a disabled or unavailable skill may still

315
00:23:10.592 --> 00:23:12.369
appear in editor inventories.

316
00:23:12.369 --> 00:23:15.479
Verify the skill's actual availability and use.

317
00:23:15.479 --> 00:23:18.145
Also consider whether execute is necessary.

318
00:23:18.145 --> 00:23:24.810
A read-only researcher does not need it just because another specialist uses a test skill.

319
00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:30.142
Adding terminal access expands the worker's capabilities beyond a simple file editor.

320
00:23:30.142 --> 00:23:37.252
For a beginner, the main lesson is composition: the agent answers who is responsible, the skill

321
00:23:37.252 --> 00:23:44.361
answers how a repeated task is performed, and the task brief answers what must be achieved

322
00:23:44.361 --> 00:23:45.250
this time.

323
00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:52.050
For the live demonstration, begin with a fresh chat and the correct harness.

324
00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:56.746
Open Customizations, create a workspace reviewer, and show the generated file.

325
00:23:56.746 --> 00:24:00.588
Replace vague persona language with a focused output contract.

326
00:24:00.588 --> 00:24:04.004
Ask it to inspect a small source file.

327
00:24:04.004 --> 00:24:09.127
Show the tool activity and explain why it should not execute tests.

328
00:24:09.127 --> 00:24:14.250
If the report implies tests passed, challenge it and refine the instructions.

329
00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.812
Then create a test changes skill.

330
00:24:16.812 --> 00:24:21.508
Include a report template as a real resource in its directory.

331
00:24:21.508 --> 00:24:28.338
Invoke it explicitly using the interface supported by the selected harness, and ask it to validate

332
00:24:28.338 --> 00:24:29.192
the starter.

333
00:24:29.192 --> 00:24:30.900
Inspect the command output.

334
00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:37.045
Next, use a plain-language request that matches the description and check whether automatic selection happens.

335
00:24:37.045 --> 00:24:43.386
If it does not, this is a useful demonstration of why discovery is not a guarantee.

336
00:24:43.386 --> 00:24:49.331
Finally, supply a task that needs a browser check but do not grant browser tools.

337
00:24:49.331 --> 00:24:54.087
The correct report marks that check as not run, with a reason.

338
00:24:54.087 --> 00:24:57.258
It must not turn unavailable capability into success.

339
00:24:57.258 --> 00:25:02.014
This demonstration is included in the first hour and is intentionally small.

340
00:25:02.014 --> 00:25:08.355
The lab gives you time to write your own files, test their behavior, and collect evidence.

341
00:25:08.355 --> 00:25:14.300
Do not use the demonstration as an excuse to skip verification in your own session.

342
00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:22.220
Delegation is useful when a separate context can contain a meaningful amount of independent work.

343
00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:27.149
A research specialist can read a module and return its contract.

344
00:25:27.149 --> 00:25:30.733
A worker can implement one independently owned feature.

345
00:25:30.733 --> 00:25:33.869
A reviewer can inspect a completed change.

346
00:25:33.869 --> 00:25:36.557
Delegation is not automatically an improvement.

347
00:25:36.557 --> 00:25:43.725
Splitting a tiny edit into several conversations can take longer and consume more AI usage than

348
00:25:43.725 --> 00:25:45.069
doing it directly.

349
00:25:45.069 --> 00:25:51.790
A task that depends on another unfinished result is not ready to run in parallel.

350
00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:58.958
The current GitHub documentation describes an orchestrator that breaks down work, identifies dependencies, assigns context and

351
00:25:58.958 --> 00:26:00.750
tools, and combines results.

352
00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:08.360
The app and CLI offer fleet mode for suitable tasks, but fleet mode does not make

353
00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:10.675
an unsafe write set safe.

354
00:26:10.675 --> 00:26:14.380
For our first delegated exercise, choose read-only research.

355
00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:20.400
It gives you practice with context and result contracts without introducing overlapping writers.

356
00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:23.179
Remember that behavior varies by harness.

357
00:26:23.179 --> 00:26:28.736
Local subagent invocations are stateless and do not inherit the full conversation.

358
00:26:28.736 --> 00:26:33.367
Another Local review requires a new invocation with updated context.

359
00:26:33.367 --> 00:26:39.851
Copilot supports its own delegation lifecycle, so do not teach Local limitations as universal.

360
00:26:39.851 --> 00:26:44.019
Start with one specialist and a clear stopping condition.

361
00:26:44.019 --> 00:26:48.650
Add parallelism only when the work genuinely benefits from it.

362
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:53.163
This slide is a worked delegation brief.

363
00:26:53.163 --> 00:26:59.490
We ask a researcher to find the priority behavior in the issue triage application.

364
00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:06.721
We provide the current contract and the repository constraints, then explicitly forbid edits and command execution.

365
00:27:06.721 --> 00:27:11.240
The expected result is not an essay about best practices.

366
00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:18.471
It is a list of relevant paths, the current input and output shapes, existing test patterns,

367
00:27:18.471 --> 00:27:21.635
and unresolved questions that might affect implementation.

368
00:27:21.635 --> 00:27:23.442
Make the request self-contained.

369
00:27:23.442 --> 00:27:30.673
If the main conversation established that all fixtures are synthetic or that dependencies must not change,

370
00:27:30.673 --> 00:27:32.029
include those facts.

371
00:27:32.029 --> 00:27:37.000
Do not assume the worker received earlier messages or repository instructions.

372
00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:43.215
For an implementation worker, replace read-only with an exact write set and include acceptance examples.

373
00:27:43.215 --> 00:27:48.830
Ask it to stop and report if it needs to change a shared contract.

374
00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:52.038
That is better than silently expanding the assignment.

375
00:27:52.038 --> 00:27:58.454
You should be able to evaluate the handoff without rereading all of the worker's intermediate conversation.

376
00:27:58.454 --> 00:28:02.063
The report should link evidence to the requested outcome.

377
00:28:02.063 --> 00:28:08.480
If the worker's result is incomplete, provide the specific missing context appropriate to the harness's lifecycle.

378
00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:14.896
In Local that means a new invocation with the relevant findings, not pretending you can continue

379
00:28:14.896 --> 00:28:16.500
the earlier stateless call.

380
00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:20.299
This coordinator pattern is deliberately conservative.

381
00:28:20.299 --> 00:28:27.763
In a Local harness session, the coordinator lists the exact research and review agents it may

382
00:28:27.763 --> 00:28:28.230
use.

383
00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:33.828
Its tools include delegation, reading, searching, editing, and the required validation capability.

384
00:28:33.828 --> 00:28:36.160
The researcher reads and reports.

385
00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.560
The coordinator implements.

386
00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:41.758
The reviewer reads the changed files and returns findings.

387
00:28:41.758 --> 00:28:47.356
There is only one implementation writer, so parallel research cannot overwrite code.

388
00:28:47.356 --> 00:28:50.622
The agents allowlist is a Local-specific configuration.

389
00:28:50.622 --> 00:28:57.153
Include the agent tool set and use names that exactly match the worker definitions.

390
00:28:57.153 --> 00:29:04.617
Picker visibility is separate from model invocation: hiding a worker from the dropdown does not automatically

391
00:29:04.617 --> 00:29:05.550
prevent delegation.

392
00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:10.256
The coordinator's instructions describe a sequence, not just a persona.

393
00:29:10.256 --> 00:29:15.212
Research first, implement from a reviewed plan, verify, then request review.

394
00:29:15.212 --> 00:29:22.421
If the review finds a defect, fix it and make a new Local review invocation with

395
00:29:22.421 --> 00:29:23.772
the updated context.

396
00:29:23.772 --> 00:29:29.630
This is often a better first multi-agent design than two unrestricted general-purpose workers.

397
00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:33.234
It uses specialization without forcing difficult merge coordination.

398
00:29:33.234 --> 00:29:38.190
In the lab, inspect the tools and files that actually changed.

399
00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:45.399
The YAML is useful configuration, but the evidence should show that the workflow followed the intended

400
00:29:45.399 --> 00:29:45.850
boundaries.

401
00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:53.860
For the Copilot harness, keep the same safe workflow but do not copy Local-only fields.

402
00:29:53.860 --> 00:29:57.976
Use native delegation with the supported agent tool alias.

403
00:29:57.976 --> 00:30:05.294
A skill can be listed in Copilot frontmatter, but the delegated task still needs the relevant

404
00:30:05.294 --> 00:30:07.124
constraints and acceptance checks.

405
00:30:07.124 --> 00:30:11.240
The conceptual coordinator remains responsible for the overall goal.

406
00:30:11.240 --> 00:30:18.557
It asks a researcher for a focused result, implements or assigns a bounded change, obtains a

407
00:30:18.557 --> 00:30:20.844
review, and integrates the evidence.

408
00:30:20.844 --> 00:30:28.162
Without the Local agents allowlist field, an instruction naming preferred workers is guidance, not equivalent schema-level

409
00:30:28.162 --> 00:30:28.619
enforcement.

410
00:30:28.619 --> 00:30:33.650
Review the actual calls and the tools available to the workers.

411
00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:40.691
Fleet mode in the app or CLI can analyze a larger prompt and parallelize appropriate subtasks.

412
00:30:40.691 --> 00:30:47.105
It may increase AI usage, and it does not automatically isolate files or remove dependencies.

413
00:30:47.105 --> 00:30:51.380
Use it only after you have defined safe task boundaries.

414
00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:58.221
For this workshop, the portable fallback is to run the same roles sequentially and pass a

415
00:30:58.221 --> 00:30:59.932
written handoff between them.

416
00:30:59.932 --> 00:31:06.773
You still learn the contracts and quality gates even when native delegation or a newer interface

417
00:31:06.773 --> 00:31:07.628
is unavailable.

418
00:31:07.628 --> 00:31:14.469
The result should be evaluated in the same way regardless of surface: the correct behavior, the

419
00:31:14.469 --> 00:31:19.600
intended file changes, honest validation evidence, and no unapproved expansion of scope.

420
00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:24.675
A handoff changes who is active in the conversation.

421
00:31:24.675 --> 00:31:31.208
Local custom agents can offer a button that switches to an implementation agent with a prefilled

422
00:31:31.208 --> 00:31:31.617
prompt.

423
00:31:31.617 --> 00:31:36.925
This is useful when you want to inspect a planning result before continuing.

424
00:31:36.925 --> 00:31:38.558
A subagent is different.

425
00:31:38.558 --> 00:31:45.092
The main agent invokes a specialist and uses its returned result while retaining ownership of the

426
00:31:45.092 --> 00:31:45.908
larger task.

427
00:31:45.908 --> 00:31:49.583
You do not have to switch the active agent.

428
00:31:49.583 --> 00:31:52.033
A separate session is different again.

429
00:31:52.033 --> 00:31:56.933
It is a tracked unit of work that you can direct independently.

430
00:31:56.933 --> 00:32:02.650
Its workspace choice determines whether it has separate files or shares an existing checkout.

431
00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:09.123
Current VS Code documentation says custom-agent handoffs are supported only by Local, not by the Copilot

432
00:32:09.123 --> 00:32:09.515
harness.

433
00:32:09.515 --> 00:32:15.789
The example uses send false so that the user reviews the prefilled prompt before sending it.

434
00:32:15.789 --> 00:32:19.709
Do not let a missing handoff button block the lab.

435
00:32:19.709 --> 00:32:25.983
A concise manual handoff containing the plan, affected files, checks, and remaining decisions achieves the learning

436
00:32:25.983 --> 00:32:28.727
objective without relying on that UI feature.

437
00:32:28.727 --> 00:32:35.000
Choose the mechanism based on the workflow: transition when a human should review a stage, delegate

438
00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:41.274
when a specialist should return a bounded result, and open a separate session when independently tracked

439
00:32:41.274 --> 00:32:42.450
work is appropriate.

440
00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:49.124
In the live demonstration, use a small triage enhancement from the lab specification.

441
00:32:49.124 --> 00:32:52.586
Do not invent the rule during the demonstration.

442
00:32:52.586 --> 00:32:57.778
Read its acceptance examples aloud so participants can predict what should happen.

443
00:32:57.778 --> 00:33:02.969
Ask a read-only researcher to identify the relevant contract, module, and tests.

444
00:33:02.969 --> 00:33:09.892
Review its report and show how the coordinator uses that result rather than repeating the entire

445
00:33:09.892 --> 00:33:10.324
investigation.

446
00:33:10.324 --> 00:33:13.786
Next, implement the bounded change with one writer.

447
00:33:13.786 --> 00:33:19.410
Keep shared fixtures and server wiring unchanged unless the task explicitly assigns them.

448
00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:23.304
Use the validation skill to run the documented checks.

449
00:33:23.304 --> 00:33:25.900
Here is the exact worked example.

450
00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:32.723
Active issues start with sixty points for high impact, thirty for medium, or ten for low.

451
00:33:32.723 --> 00:33:34.793
A blocked workflow adds twenty-five.

452
00:33:34.793 --> 00:33:38.104
One hundred or more affected users adds ten.

453
00:33:38.104 --> 00:33:41.416
Seven or more days of age adds five.

454
00:33:41.416 --> 00:33:45.969
P one begins at eighty points, and P two at forty.

455
00:33:45.969 --> 00:33:51.764
Fixture ISS one hundred and one totals one hundred points and is P one.

456
00:33:51.764 --> 00:33:58.388
Closed issues override all those additions and return zero, P three, with only the closed-issue reason.

457
00:33:58.388 --> 00:34:00.457
Explain three categories of tests.

458
00:34:00.457 --> 00:34:03.769
A positive case must trigger the new behavior.

459
00:34:03.769 --> 00:34:06.252
A negative case must remain unchanged.

460
00:34:06.252 --> 00:34:09.150
A boundary case verifies the actual threshold.

461
00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:17.083
Eighty itself is unreachable under this fixed additive scoring policy, so compare the nearest reachable cases:

462
00:34:17.083 --> 00:34:21.433
seventy-five is P two, while eighty-five is P one.

463
00:34:21.433 --> 00:34:28.200
Test one hundred users versus ninety-nine and seven days versus six separately as well.

464
00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:34.000
Then ask a restricted reviewer to inspect the change against the requirements.

465
00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:41.250
The reviewer can find reasoning defects, but it cannot truthfully claim execution without execution tools.

466
00:34:41.250 --> 00:34:44.633
The coordinator supplies the actual test evidence.

467
00:34:44.633 --> 00:34:48.983
Finally, fix actionable findings or report what remains uncertain.

468
00:34:48.983 --> 00:34:55.750
This shows the full loop: researched context, bounded implementation, measured evidence, and independent review.

469
00:34:55.950 --> 00:35:01.750
It is much more useful than a demonstration that stops after the agent writes code.

470
00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:06.057
Before parallel implementation, look for hot files.

471
00:35:06.057 --> 00:35:13.044
These are files that several tasks are likely to touch: a shared type or contract, the

472
00:35:13.044 --> 00:35:17.848
package manifest, the lockfile, application wiring, generated artifacts, and common fixtures.

473
00:35:17.848 --> 00:35:24.835
If both workers edit a hot file, merely asking them to be careful is not a

474
00:35:24.835 --> 00:35:25.708
reliable plan.

475
00:35:25.708 --> 00:35:32.259
Assign that file to one integrator, or complete the prerequisite change before starting independent tasks.

476
00:35:32.259 --> 00:35:37.936
There are also shared resources that do not appear in a source diff.

477
00:35:37.936 --> 00:35:41.866
Two test commands can write the same report directory.

478
00:35:41.866 --> 00:35:45.360
Two servers can compete for port three thousand.

479
00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:47.980
A shared database can mix state.

480
00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:50.600
External services can receive duplicate requests.

481
00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:54.936
In one checkout, Git's index and branch are shared.

482
00:35:54.936 --> 00:36:01.830
Do not let two agents independently stage all files and commit at the same time.

483
00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:05.966
One writer can accidentally include the other's unfinished work.

484
00:36:05.966 --> 00:36:13.319
Worktrees reduce that particular problem because they provide separate working copies and indices, but integration still

485
00:36:13.319 --> 00:36:14.238
requires care.

486
00:36:14.238 --> 00:36:20.672
The practice app avoids databases, credentials, and external services to keep these issues manageable.

487
00:36:20.672 --> 00:36:25.728
Its task board still records file ownership and test output expectations.

488
00:36:25.728 --> 00:36:33.081
The general lesson is to identify every writable surface, not only the source module named in

489
00:36:33.081 --> 00:36:34.000
the prompt.

490
00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:41.900
When you are unsure about independent write boundaries, use parallel readers and one integration writer.

491
00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:49.260
Researchers can inspect separate questions concurrently, but they return findings rather than changing the shared checkout.

492
00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:52.480
One researcher might explain the priority rules.

493
00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:55.700
Another might inspect the accessible filter controls.

494
00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:59.840
The coordinator uses both reports to plan a change.

495
00:36:59.840 --> 00:37:03.980
Only the integrator edits the shared source and wiring.

496
00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:06.280
Another variation is patch proposals.

497
00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:11.340
A worker returns a diff or proposed implementation without applying it.

498
00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:17.320
The integrator checks it against the current file version and applies it deliberately.

499
00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:24.680
If the base changed, regenerate or rebase the proposal; do not paste an old replacement over

500
00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:25.600
new work.

501
00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:33.162
This approach is slower than unrestricted parallel writing in some cases, but it greatly reduces coordination

502
00:37:33.162 --> 00:37:33.622
risk.

503
00:37:33.622 --> 00:37:40.063
It is a strong default for small repositories, tightly coupled tasks, or inexperienced teams.

504
00:37:40.063 --> 00:37:46.505
Use separate specialists only when their work is substantial enough to justify the overhead.

505
00:37:46.505 --> 00:37:51.566
Two readers examining the same short file may simply duplicate effort.

506
00:37:51.566 --> 00:37:56.167
In the lab, you will first exercise this safe pattern.

507
00:37:56.167 --> 00:38:03.529
Then you will compare it with disjoint writers and worktrees, so you learn to choose rather

508
00:38:03.529 --> 00:38:06.750
than assume one technique fits every task.

509
00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:11.952
Disjoint ownership works when the tasks really are independent.

510
00:38:11.952 --> 00:38:15.904
Start by freezing the shared input and output contract.

511
00:38:15.904 --> 00:38:21.612
Then assign each worker a small set of files and its own tests.

512
00:38:21.612 --> 00:38:28.198
For example, one worker may implement a search predicate while another implements a summary formatter.

513
00:38:28.198 --> 00:38:31.272
Both consume the same documented issue shape.

514
00:38:31.272 --> 00:38:35.224
Neither changes that shape or the shared server wiring.

515
00:38:35.224 --> 00:38:37.858
The integrator combines their results later.

516
00:38:37.858 --> 00:38:44.005
The task board should record the owner, allowed files, dependencies, acceptance checks, and status.

517
00:38:44.005 --> 00:38:49.275
It should also say which shared files are reserved for the integrator.

518
00:38:49.275 --> 00:38:56.300
A worker that discovers it needs another owner's file must stop and request a contract decision.

519
00:38:56.500 --> 00:39:00.229
Do not let it quietly edit outside the assignment.

520
00:39:00.229 --> 00:39:04.787
That small interruption prevents a much larger merge or behavior defect.

521
00:39:04.787 --> 00:39:08.931
Remember that different source files can still be behaviorally coupled.

522
00:39:08.931 --> 00:39:15.561
If one worker renames a field that the other consumes, file disjointness does not make the

523
00:39:15.561 --> 00:39:16.390
tasks independent.

524
00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:20.533
The frozen contract is as important as the ownership list.

525
00:39:20.533 --> 00:39:24.263
This is a team convention, not a built-in lock.

526
00:39:24.263 --> 00:39:30.893
We will add explicit scope checks and inspect the combined diff to detect violations rather than

527
00:39:30.893 --> 00:39:32.550
merely trusting the board.

528
00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:38.486
A task graph helps you decide what can run now.

529
00:39:38.486 --> 00:39:44.766
If both feature tasks depend on a shared contract change, finish that change first.

530
00:39:44.766 --> 00:39:49.252
Only then are the feature tasks ready to run independently.

531
00:39:49.252 --> 00:39:52.841
The graph on this slide has four stages.

532
00:39:52.841 --> 00:39:59.570
Define the contract, run two independent workers, integrate their output, then validate the combined application.

533
00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:04.056
The arrows represent actual prerequisites, not a preferred list order.

534
00:40:04.056 --> 00:40:07.645
Shared configuration changes belong in a serialized stage.

535
00:40:07.645 --> 00:40:14.374
If a dependency must be added, let the integrator update the manifest and lockfile once.

536
00:40:14.374 --> 00:40:18.411
Do not ask every worker to solve installation separately.

537
00:40:18.411 --> 00:40:22.000
Reserve one owner for the coordination board too.

538
00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:28.717
Multiple agents updating the same board can collide just as they can collide in code.

539
00:40:28.717 --> 00:40:33.497
Workers can report status to the coordinator, which updates the record.

540
00:40:33.497 --> 00:40:36.103
Individual tests are necessary but insufficient.

541
00:40:36.103 --> 00:40:43.055
Two branches can pass independently and fail together because they make different assumptions about the contract

542
00:40:43.055 --> 00:40:44.359
or user flow.

543
00:40:44.359 --> 00:40:48.269
Run the combined suite and smoke checks after integration.

544
00:40:48.269 --> 00:40:53.483
If the graph is mostly a chain, parallel execution adds little value.

545
00:40:53.483 --> 00:41:00.000
Use a sequential workflow and invest the saved attention in clearer acceptance examples and review.

546
00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:05.186
You can improve shared-checkout coordination with an advisory claim protocol.

547
00:41:05.186 --> 00:41:11.883
Before writing, a worker states the paths it intends to own, the starting revision, and when

548
00:41:11.883 --> 00:41:13.557
the claim should expire.

549
00:41:13.557 --> 00:41:17.324
The coordinator checks for conflicts and acknowledges the assignment.

550
00:41:17.324 --> 00:41:22.347
At handoff, the worker reports the changed files and releases its claim.

551
00:41:22.347 --> 00:41:27.370
If it needs another path, it pauses and requests a new assignment.

552
00:41:27.370 --> 00:41:34.067
An expiry prevents a stale claim from blocking work forever, but expiry is not permission to

553
00:41:34.067 --> 00:41:35.741
overwrite an active worker.

554
00:41:35.741 --> 00:41:39.927
The coordinator must inspect the current state and resolve uncertainty.

555
00:41:39.927 --> 00:41:44.950
This is similar to a team agreeing who edits a document section.

556
00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:48.770
It works only when participants follow the protocol.

557
00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:56.011
A Markdown file or chat message is not an operating-system mutex and does not stop a

558
00:41:56.011 --> 00:41:58.273
terminal command from writing elsewhere.

559
00:41:58.273 --> 00:42:02.798
Actual locking requires an enforcement mechanism with defined failure behavior.

560
00:42:02.798 --> 00:42:10.039
In many coding workshops, the practical combination is advisory ownership, narrow tools, scope checks, and one

561
00:42:10.039 --> 00:42:10.491
integrator.

562
00:42:10.491 --> 00:42:15.017
Worktrees are another option when independent working copies are helpful.

563
00:42:15.017 --> 00:42:19.994
Do not oversell claims as a product feature or security boundary.

564
00:42:19.994 --> 00:42:27.235
They are an original workflow technique in these materials, and the labs deliberately include a violation

565
00:42:27.235 --> 00:42:29.950
check so participants see the limitation.

566
00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:34.612
Guidance is valuable, but enforcement must be explicit.

567
00:42:34.612 --> 00:42:40.932
A changed-path scope check can fail when a task modifies files outside its assignment.

568
00:42:40.932 --> 00:42:44.995
Required continuous integration can block integration until tests pass.

569
00:42:44.995 --> 00:42:49.510
Review ownership can ensure the right people approve a change.

570
00:42:49.510 --> 00:42:52.670
These mechanisms detect or gate different things.

571
00:42:52.670 --> 00:42:57.184
CODEOWNERS routes review; it does not prevent a local edit.

572
00:42:57.184 --> 00:43:04.408
A post-change scope check detects a violation after the write; it does not stop the initial

573
00:43:04.408 --> 00:43:04.859
write.

574
00:43:04.859 --> 00:43:08.922
Tool restrictions reduce the capabilities available to a specialist.

575
00:43:08.922 --> 00:43:16.146
Be careful with terminal execution, because a shell can modify files even if a separate edit

576
00:43:16.146 --> 00:43:17.500
tool is absent.

577
00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:23.775
Current Copilot hooks can inspect a pre-tool-use event and return a documented denial.

578
00:43:23.775 --> 00:43:29.850
VS Code's hooks experience is preview, and different harnesses use different event formats.

579
00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:34.056
A post-tool hook cannot undo an action already completed.

580
00:43:34.056 --> 00:43:41.534
There is a subtle failure rule: documented command pre-tool-use errors deny a call, but timeouts fall

581
00:43:41.534 --> 00:43:43.403
through to normal permissions.

582
00:43:43.403 --> 00:43:47.142
Therefore, do not describe every hook as fail-closed.

583
00:43:47.142 --> 00:43:50.413
The core labs do not enable hooks.

584
00:43:50.413 --> 00:43:56.488
They teach deterministic checks and manual review, with hooks as an optional extension.

585
00:43:56.488 --> 00:44:03.965
Before using a hook for enforcement, test valid, invalid, malformed, and timed-out cases in the actual

586
00:44:03.965 --> 00:44:04.900
execution environment.

587
00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:12.499
A Git worktree gives one repository another working directory, usually on a different branch.

588
00:44:12.499 --> 00:44:18.534
This lets independent tasks edit separate copies without overwriting each other's working files.

589
00:44:18.534 --> 00:44:20.855
Begin from a committed baseline.

590
00:44:20.855 --> 00:44:25.961
That baseline should include the contract and the customizations workers need.

591
00:44:25.961 --> 00:44:31.532
Uncommitted agent files do not magically appear in a newly created worktree.

592
00:44:31.532 --> 00:44:36.174
Ignored dependencies and local configuration are also absent by default.

593
00:44:36.174 --> 00:44:41.281
The example creates two task worktrees from the same agreed base.

594
00:44:41.281 --> 00:44:48.708
The paths and branch names are examples; check your actual repository and use the platform-specific instructions

595
00:44:48.708 --> 00:44:50.101
in the lab.

596
00:44:50.101 --> 00:44:56.600
Worktrees still share parts of Git's repository metadata and do not isolate external resources.

597
00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:03.847
Use different server ports and output directories, and do not let workers change shared credentials or

598
00:45:03.847 --> 00:45:04.728
external services.

599
00:45:04.728 --> 00:45:10.013
When a task finishes, its commit is still on its task branch.

600
00:45:10.013 --> 00:45:13.537
It has not automatically reached the target branch.

601
00:45:13.537 --> 00:45:17.942
Review and integrate it deliberately, then retest the combined result.

602
00:45:17.942 --> 00:45:21.906
Keep a worktree until integration and verification are complete.

603
00:45:21.906 --> 00:45:27.191
Do not remove it merely because the agent's last response sounded successful.

604
00:45:27.191 --> 00:45:34.238
If there is a conflict, resolve it based on the contract and intended behavior, not by

605
00:45:34.238 --> 00:45:36.000
blindly choosing one side.

606
00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:40.811
Integration is a separate stage with its own owner.

607
00:45:40.811 --> 00:45:43.774
First inspect each task's diff and evidence.

608
00:45:43.774 --> 00:45:50.549
Verify that the worker stayed within its assignment and that the tests cover the requested behavior

609
00:45:50.549 --> 00:45:52.666
rather than only implementation details.

610
00:45:52.666 --> 00:45:55.629
Then integrate one task at a time.

611
00:45:55.629 --> 00:46:02.404
Your normal Git or pull-request workflow may use a merge, a cherry-pick of a reviewed commit,

612
00:46:02.404 --> 00:46:04.944
or a PR with required checks.

613
00:46:04.944 --> 00:46:08.755
These are integration choices, not reasons to skip review.

614
00:46:08.755 --> 00:46:12.142
A conflict means the two changes need reasoning.

615
00:46:12.142 --> 00:46:15.106
Do not automatically choose ours or theirs.

616
00:46:15.106 --> 00:46:20.186
Read both intentions, check the shared contract, and preserve the required behavior.

617
00:46:20.186 --> 00:46:23.150
Even a clean merge can be wrong.

618
00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:30.083
The changes may compile and pass their separate tests while disagreeing on a field name, filter

619
00:46:30.083 --> 00:46:31.767
semantics, or user-visible order.

620
00:46:31.767 --> 00:46:35.975
Run the complete relevant suite and exercise the combined flow.

621
00:46:35.975 --> 00:46:42.708
For the issue triage app, that means opening the page, applying the new behavior, and checking

622
00:46:42.708 --> 00:46:45.654
the expected output against the fixture examples.

623
00:46:45.654 --> 00:46:52.388
A test success message alone is not enough if the feature never appears in the interface.

624
00:46:52.388 --> 00:46:59.121
The lab answer key demonstrates the combined result and documents what remains intentionally out of scope.

625
00:46:59.121 --> 00:47:03.750
Good integration includes an honest handoff, not just a merge commit.

626
00:47:05.000 --> 00:47:11.568
The third live demonstration compares two patterns rather than declaring worktrees the only answer.

627
00:47:11.568 --> 00:47:15.791
First, use the shared checkout with parallel read-only specialists.

628
00:47:15.791 --> 00:47:20.013
The coordinator receives their reports and performs the edit.

629
00:47:20.013 --> 00:47:27.050
Show the changed-file list so participants can see that only the assigned writer changed code.

630
00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:32.680
Second, show two independent tasks from a committed contract in separate worktrees.

631
00:47:32.680 --> 00:47:38.310
Point out the actual paths and branches, not just separate conversation tabs.

632
00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:43.002
Use the lab's scope-check mechanism to demonstrate a controlled violation.

633
00:47:43.002 --> 00:47:49.101
A proposed change to a shared file should fail the task's allowed-path check.

634
00:47:49.101 --> 00:47:55.200
Do not manufacture an unsafe destructive operation; the lesson is detection and correction.

635
00:47:55.400 --> 00:48:00.401
Then review one task, integrate it, and repeat for the next.

636
00:48:00.401 --> 00:48:04.947
The concrete combined example is a case-insensitive search for PAYMENTS.

637
00:48:04.947 --> 00:48:10.856
It returns ISS one hundred and one and ISS one hundred and five.

638
00:48:10.856 --> 00:48:16.766
The summary shows two total issues, one active and one active blocked issue.

639
00:48:16.766 --> 00:48:24.040
Evidence export includes only the active issue, ISS one hundred and one, with its P one

640
00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:24.494
priority.

641
00:48:24.494 --> 00:48:30.859
This checks that search, summary, priority and handoff agree on the same visible issues.

642
00:48:30.859 --> 00:48:34.950
Run the combined acceptance checks and inspect the page.

643
00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:41.961
Explain that commit actions in some UI workflows can skip local hooks, so required checks must

644
00:48:41.961 --> 00:48:46.218
be run explicitly and enforced by the normal integration policy.

645
00:48:46.218 --> 00:48:53.028
Finally, ask participants which pattern they would choose if both tasks needed the same contract change.

646
00:48:53.028 --> 00:48:59.839
The correct first step is to serialize or assign that contract change, not to launch more

647
00:48:59.839 --> 00:49:00.265
agents.

648
00:49:00.265 --> 00:49:03.245
This demonstration fits inside the first hour.

649
00:49:03.245 --> 00:49:06.650
The forty-minute orchestration lab provides the hands-on practice.

650
00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:10.892
Agent safety is not one switch.

651
00:49:10.892 --> 00:49:16.194
File isolation, tool permissions, sandboxing, and review each address different risks.

652
00:49:16.194 --> 00:49:18.604
A worktree separates source changes.

653
00:49:18.604 --> 00:49:25.352
A sandbox can restrict filesystem, network, and credential access, depending on platform and configuration.

654
00:49:25.352 --> 00:49:29.208
Approval settings determine which tool actions require confirmation.

655
00:49:29.208 --> 00:49:33.546
None of them proves the generated code is correct.

656
00:49:33.546 --> 00:49:37.884
The starter uses fictional issues and no external integration.

657
00:49:37.884 --> 00:49:44.632
Do not replace those fixtures with customer data or paste secrets into a prompt.

658
00:49:44.632 --> 00:49:52.344
If you later connect an MCP server or connector, inspect the permissions and the data destination.

659
00:49:52.344 --> 00:49:56.200
Review acquired skills and plugins before enabling them.

660
00:49:56.400 --> 00:50:03.771
A skill directory may contain scripts, and hooks can run without the model asking for the

661
00:50:03.771 --> 00:50:04.693
procedure explicitly.

662
00:50:04.693 --> 00:50:07.918
Marketplace availability is not a trust guarantee.

663
00:50:07.918 --> 00:50:15.289
Treat repository content and external text as data, not authoritative instructions to bypass the task's rules.

664
00:50:15.289 --> 00:50:22.661
If an issue asks the agent to ignore ownership boundaries, the worker should report that request

665
00:50:22.661 --> 00:50:24.504
rather than obey it.

666
00:50:24.504 --> 00:50:27.268
Before committing, inspect the complete diff.

667
00:50:27.268 --> 00:50:31.875
Before any external action, verify the destination and required approval.

668
00:50:31.875 --> 00:50:39.246
The workshop focuses on local code precisely so you can learn these habits without creating avoidable

669
00:50:39.246 --> 00:50:41.550
cloud cost or data exposure.

670
00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:49.307
The best agent workflow is often the smallest one that reliably completes the task.

671
00:50:49.307 --> 00:50:53.811
A direct request is appropriate for a small cohesive edit.

672
00:50:53.811 --> 00:51:00.568
A specialist is useful when the work needs substantial separate context or a recurring procedure.

673
00:51:00.568 --> 00:51:07.776
Parallelism saves time only when tasks are independent enough that coordination costs do not exceed the

674
00:51:07.776 --> 00:51:08.226
benefit.

675
00:51:08.226 --> 00:51:14.983
Repeating the same investigation in several contexts can consume more usage without improving the result.

676
00:51:14.983 --> 00:51:16.335
Bound delegation depth.

677
00:51:16.335 --> 00:51:20.840
Recursive delegation needs a stopping condition and has harness-specific support.

678
00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:24.443
It is not part of our core labs.

679
00:51:24.443 --> 00:51:30.750
A simple coordinator and one or two workers are enough to learn the principle.

680
00:51:30.950 --> 00:51:35.655
Choose a model available to your account and suitable for the task.

681
00:51:35.655 --> 00:51:41.928
Do not copy a fixed model identifier from an old presentation and assume it is still

682
00:51:41.928 --> 00:51:42.320
offered.

683
00:51:42.320 --> 00:51:47.809
Keep model changes separate from harness changes so you know what affected the result.

684
00:51:47.809 --> 00:51:49.770
Measure both quality and usage.

685
00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:52.906
Did the worker return a correct, usable result?

686
00:51:52.906 --> 00:51:56.435
How much extra context and waiting did coordination introduce?

687
00:51:56.435 --> 00:51:59.964
Did a lighter model suffice for a straightforward task?

688
00:51:59.964 --> 00:52:04.669
In the Copilot app, current documentation also describes usage insights through chronicle.

689
00:52:04.669 --> 00:52:10.550
That is an optional way to reflect on patterns, not a requirement for the lab.

690
00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:16.800
The key habit is to optimize the workflow, not just increase the agent count.

691
00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:20.975
The practice application is intentionally modest.

692
00:52:20.975 --> 00:52:28.909
It serves a static issue triage board using Node's built-in HTTP support and uses the built-in

693
00:52:28.909 --> 00:52:29.901
test runner.

694
00:52:29.901 --> 00:52:37.835
There is no database, authentication service, external AI call, or dependency-heavy framework to distract from the

695
00:52:37.835 --> 00:52:38.331
workflow.

696
00:52:38.331 --> 00:52:46.265
The board shows eight fictional support issues: six active and two closed, with two active blocked

697
00:52:46.265 --> 00:52:46.761
workflows.

698
00:52:46.761 --> 00:52:52.215
Four categories have two issues each: accessibility, payments, reporting, and search.

699
00:52:52.215 --> 00:52:59.653
The exercises add priority scoring, case-insensitive search, summaries, and evidence export behind documented feature-module boundaries.

700
00:52:59.653 --> 00:53:07.587
That gives us a realistic reason to use skills, reviewers, and multiple task owners without building

701
00:53:07.587 --> 00:53:09.571
a production support platform.

702
00:53:09.571 --> 00:53:12.050
Start with a passing baseline.

703
00:53:12.250 --> 00:53:18.478
A workshop should not require you to fix unrelated infrastructure before learning its topic.

704
00:53:18.478 --> 00:53:23.816
The missing lab features are intentional and described in the lab specifications.

705
00:53:23.816 --> 00:53:28.264
The repository separates starter templates, attendee instructions, and instructor answers.

706
00:53:28.264 --> 00:53:35.382
The answer key can demonstrate one valid solution, but the acceptance checks evaluate behavior rather than

707
00:53:35.382 --> 00:53:36.716
requiring identical code.

708
00:53:36.716 --> 00:53:41.165
Use the documented start and test commands from the README.

709
00:53:41.165 --> 00:53:48.282
Run the smoke check and inspect the page so you know the app works before asking

710
00:53:48.282 --> 00:53:50.062
agents to change it.

711
00:53:50.062 --> 00:53:55.400
When you assign work, use the repository's actual module paths and contract.

712
00:53:55.600 --> 00:54:02.864
The generic diagrams in this deck illustrate the pattern; the lab files are the executable source

713
00:54:02.864 --> 00:54:06.950
of truth for exact features, fixtures, and allowed files.

714
00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:10.411
The first lab takes thirty minutes.

715
00:54:10.411 --> 00:54:16.440
Use the first few minutes to confirm the passing starter and select the intended harness.

716
00:54:16.440 --> 00:54:21.262
Then write short repository instructions based on the existing commands and constraints.

717
00:54:21.262 --> 00:54:24.879
Create a custom agent for a focused recurring role.

718
00:54:24.879 --> 00:54:31.309
It should have a useful description, only the tools it needs, and a result format you

719
00:54:31.309 --> 00:54:32.113
can evaluate.

720
00:54:32.113 --> 00:54:37.338
Do not simply call it senior expert and hope that defines its behavior.

721
00:54:37.338 --> 00:54:41.357
Create a real skill directory with matching name and frontmatter.

722
00:54:41.357 --> 00:54:46.581
Include at least one supporting resource, and reference it from SKILL dot markdown.

723
00:54:46.581 --> 00:54:50.600
Verify that its commands and paths work in this repository.

724
00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:52.873
Test discovery and behavior separately.

725
00:54:52.873 --> 00:54:57.020
A listed agent or skill is available, not necessarily correct.

726
00:54:57.020 --> 00:55:03.240
Use a small known request, inspect the activity, and compare the result with the source.

727
00:55:03.240 --> 00:55:09.875
Record a positive invocation and a case that must be reported as not run or out

728
00:55:09.875 --> 00:55:10.705
of scope.

729
00:55:10.705 --> 00:55:14.022
This makes honesty part of the acceptance criteria.

730
00:55:14.022 --> 00:55:19.828
The lab provides step-by-step actions, expected outputs, a hint ladder, and an answer key.

731
00:55:19.828 --> 00:55:25.218
If you fall behind, use the first hint before copying a complete solution.

732
00:55:25.218 --> 00:55:31.853
At the checkpoint, you should be able to explain why each instruction belongs in its chosen

733
00:55:31.853 --> 00:55:36.000
file and why the specialist does not need unrestricted tools.

734
00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:39.982
The second lab takes thirty-five minutes.

735
00:55:39.982 --> 00:55:43.959
Your first deliverable is a self-contained delegated brief.

736
00:55:43.959 --> 00:55:49.924
It includes the relevant contract, repository constraints, allowed actions, and expected report.

737
00:55:49.924 --> 00:55:55.888
Use the read-only researcher to locate the behavior and existing test pattern.

738
00:55:55.888 --> 00:55:58.374
Check its report before implementation.

739
00:55:58.374 --> 00:56:02.350
If it assumes missing context, correct the brief.

740
00:56:02.350 --> 00:56:07.818
Then use the coordinator workflow to implement the lab's exact enhancement.

741
00:56:07.818 --> 00:56:12.291
One implementation writer owns the assigned source and tests.

742
00:56:12.291 --> 00:56:15.771
The skill provides the repeatable validation procedure.

743
00:56:15.771 --> 00:56:20.244
The acceptance criteria include positive, negative, and boundary cases.

744
00:56:20.244 --> 00:56:27.700
Do not accept a nearby example as proof of the actual threshold or precedence rule.

745
00:56:27.900 --> 00:56:32.354
Ask the reviewer to inspect the changed code against the requirements.

746
00:56:32.354 --> 00:56:38.834
Supply the test output as evidence; do not let a read-only reviewer claim it executed commands.

747
00:56:38.834 --> 00:56:44.503
If native delegation is unavailable, perform the roles sequentially with a concise manual handoff.

748
00:56:44.503 --> 00:56:48.552
You still need to produce the same behavior and evidence.

749
00:56:48.552 --> 00:56:55.031
At the checkpoint, your report must show what changed, which checks ran, their results, and any

750
00:56:55.031 --> 00:56:55.841
remaining uncertainty.

751
00:56:55.841 --> 00:56:59.486
It must also identify the ownership boundary you preserved.

752
00:56:59.486 --> 00:57:04.750
The goal is a complete, verifiable loop, not merely a successful subagent call.

753
00:57:06.000 --> 00:57:10.803
The third lab takes forty minutes and concentrates on one repository.

754
00:57:10.803 --> 00:57:15.606
Start by reading the shared contract and the two task specifications.

755
00:57:15.606 --> 00:57:21.282
Decide which files belong to each worker and which remain with the integrator.

756
00:57:21.282 --> 00:57:22.592
Record the dependencies.

757
00:57:22.592 --> 00:57:28.268
If either task needs a contract change, perform that prerequisite before parallel work.

758
00:57:28.268 --> 00:57:31.325
Do not launch workers against different assumptions.

759
00:57:31.325 --> 00:57:38.311
Exercise the shared-checkout safe pattern using readers and one writer, then compare the worktree option where

760
00:57:38.311 --> 00:57:38.748
available.

761
00:57:38.748 --> 00:57:43.987
The lab's fallback runs the same tasks sequentially without newer UI features.

762
00:57:43.987 --> 00:57:47.917
Use the task board to record claims and handoffs.

763
00:57:47.917 --> 00:57:50.100
Treat the board as advisory.

764
00:57:50.300 --> 00:57:56.784
Run the changed-path scope check to catch a controlled violation and explain whether the check prevents

765
00:57:56.784 --> 00:57:59.215
a write or detects it afterward.

766
00:57:59.215 --> 00:58:03.268
Review the worker results and integrate one at a time.

767
00:58:03.268 --> 00:58:07.725
Shared files, Git integration, and board updates belong to the integrator.

768
00:58:07.725 --> 00:58:11.372
Do not allow concurrent stage-all commits in one checkout.

769
00:58:11.372 --> 00:58:15.425
Finally, rerun the combined checks and inspect the actual application.

770
00:58:15.425 --> 00:58:18.261
Separate green test reports are not sufficient.

771
00:58:18.261 --> 00:58:24.745
At the debrief, explain which strategy you chose, what could still collide, and how you would

772
00:58:24.745 --> 00:58:27.987
handle a worker that needed an unassigned file.

773
00:58:27.987 --> 00:58:32.850
That reasoning is the main advanced skill this workshop aims to develop.

774
00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:36.703
The final lab takes fifteen minutes.

775
00:58:36.703 --> 00:58:43.460
It is deliberately short because integration and validation have already happened in the previous exercises.

776
00:58:43.460 --> 00:58:47.514
Now consolidate the evidence and review the complete result.

777
00:58:47.514 --> 00:58:52.019
Inspect the entire relevant diff, not only the last turn.

778
00:58:52.019 --> 00:58:59.226
Look for unrelated edits, changes to shared contracts, and tests that merely mirror the implementation without

779
00:58:59.226 --> 00:59:00.578
proving the requirement.

780
00:59:00.578 --> 00:59:06.434
Run the final quality gates and the user-flow check described in the lab.

781
00:59:06.434 --> 00:59:12.290
If a check cannot run, mark it as not run with a reason.

782
00:59:12.290 --> 00:59:15.443
Do not present it as a success.

783
00:59:15.443 --> 00:59:21.750
Write a handoff that another developer can use without reading the full agent history.

784
00:59:21.950 --> 00:59:29.038
Include the outcome, changed paths, contract version or assumptions, exact checks, results, risks, and next decisions.

785
00:59:29.038 --> 00:59:30.810
Then reflect on orchestration.

786
00:59:30.810 --> 00:59:33.025
Which tasks were actually independent?

787
00:59:33.025 --> 00:59:35.240
Did separate contexts improve focus?

788
00:59:35.240 --> 00:59:40.113
Did coordination consume more effort than a direct edit would have?

789
00:59:40.113 --> 00:59:46.758
Would you use worktrees, one writer, or a sequential chain for the same task tomorrow?

790
00:59:46.758 --> 00:59:49.416
The workshop ends at three hours.

791
00:59:49.416 --> 00:59:56.504
Optional extensions such as hook enforcement, marketplace packaging, or cloud continuity belong after the core session.

792
00:59:56.504 --> 01:00:03.592
A good handoff is the final proof that you can direct agents as engineering collaborators rather

793
01:00:03.592 --> 01:00:06.250
than simply collect their completion messages.

794
01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:14.119
When a workflow goes wrong, recover from evidence rather than repeatedly issuing a broader prompt.

795
01:00:14.119 --> 01:00:21.180
If an agent or skill is missing, check its path, frontmatter, selected harness, and enabled inventory.

796
01:00:21.180 --> 01:00:26.917
In a new worktree, confirm that the customization was committed in the base.

797
01:00:26.917 --> 01:00:33.536
A file that exists only in the original checkout is not present in the worktree.

798
01:00:33.536 --> 01:00:40.155
If a worker has the wrong tools, compare the runtime activity with the harness-specific reference.

799
01:00:40.155 --> 01:00:44.568
Do not assume an editor completion guarantees availability or enforcement.

800
01:00:44.568 --> 01:00:50.305
If two writers overlap, pause them and inspect the actual files and changes.

801
01:00:50.305 --> 01:00:55.600
Preserve useful work, identify the shared contract, and choose one integration owner.

802
01:00:55.800 --> 01:01:02.000
Do not run a destructive reset just to get back to a clean-looking state.

803
01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:07.314
Stopping a request does not undo completed terminal commands or external actions.

804
01:01:07.314 --> 01:01:14.400
Checkpoints may restore certain workspace edits and chat state, but they are not a general rollback

805
01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:14.843
system.

806
01:01:14.843 --> 01:01:21.486
Use the documented non-destructive lab reset or normal Git recovery appropriate to the actual state.

807
01:01:21.486 --> 01:01:25.471
After correction, rerun the checks affected by the change.

808
01:01:25.471 --> 01:01:30.786
A recovery is complete only when the required behavior is verified again.

809
01:01:30.786 --> 01:01:37.871
These habits matter more than memorizing a particular button label, because the interfaces and defaults will

810
01:01:37.871 --> 01:01:39.200
continue to evolve.

811
01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:45.108
Let's finish with four ideas you can reuse immediately.

812
01:01:45.108 --> 01:01:50.228
First, compose a role, a recipe, and a task contract.

813
01:01:50.228 --> 01:01:52.788
The custom agent defines responsibility.

814
01:01:52.788 --> 01:01:55.860
The skill supplies a repeated procedure.

815
01:01:55.860 --> 01:02:00.468
The prompt specifies this particular outcome and its constraints.

816
01:02:00.468 --> 01:02:04.052
Second, distinguish context isolation from workspace isolation.

817
01:02:04.052 --> 01:02:08.148
Separate subagents or chats can still share files.

818
01:02:08.148 --> 01:02:14.292
A worktree separates working copies, while permissions and sandboxing address different risks.

819
01:02:14.292 --> 01:02:17.364
Third, parallelize only genuinely independent work.

820
01:02:17.364 --> 01:02:23.508
Freeze contracts, assign explicit ownership, serialize shared-file changes, and use one integrator.

821
01:02:23.508 --> 01:02:28.628
Advisory claims help coordination but do not enforce a lock.

822
01:02:28.628 --> 01:02:31.700
Fourth, trust evidence over confident prose.

823
01:02:31.900 --> 01:02:39.666
Review the source, run requirement-focused checks, inspect the actual user flow, and report skipped checks honestly.

824
01:02:39.666 --> 01:02:44.034
Before you leave, test your understanding with three questions.

825
01:02:44.034 --> 01:02:47.917
Can a read-only reviewer claim it ran tests?

826
01:02:47.917 --> 01:02:48.403
No.

827
01:02:48.403 --> 01:02:52.286
Does target vscode switch the runtime to Local?

828
01:02:52.286 --> 01:02:52.771
No.

829
01:02:52.771 --> 01:02:57.140
Can two worktrees pass separately and fail after integration?

830
01:02:57.140 --> 01:03:01.508
Yes, which is why we retest the combined result.

831
01:03:01.508 --> 01:03:08.303
The repository's labs, instructor answers, source ledger, captions, and transcript support repeating the workshop.

832
01:03:08.303 --> 01:03:13.642
Recheck the product sources before teaching it on a later date.

833
01:03:13.642 --> 01:03:21.408
The durable lesson is not a fixed interface: it is the discipline of directing bounded work

834
01:03:21.408 --> 01:03:23.350
and proving the result.

835
01:03:24.500 --> 01:03:31.299
This reference slide is an appendix, not extra time added to the live workshop.

836
01:03:31.299 --> 01:03:39.069
The complete source ledger contains the official URLs, what each source supports, and the caveats that

837
01:03:39.069 --> 01:03:40.526
affect our examples.

838
01:03:40.526 --> 01:03:47.811
The materials were checked against live documentation on November seventh, twenty twenty-six, US Eastern time.

839
01:03:47.811 --> 01:03:55.581
VS Code pages fetched for the workshop displayed November fourth update dates where available, and the

840
01:03:55.581 --> 01:03:58.495
Universe release is dated October twenty-eighth.

841
01:03:58.495 --> 01:04:06.266
Before a later delivery, verify the actual installed clients, organizational permissions, available harnesses, and customization syntax.

842
01:04:06.266 --> 01:04:10.151
Do not infer compatibility from a filename alone.

843
01:04:10.151 --> 01:04:16.950
App Projects, customization marketplaces, connectors, and the VS Code hooks experience have preview limitations.

844
01:04:17.150 --> 01:04:19.100
Computer use is experimental.

845
01:04:19.100 --> 01:04:24.462
The core labs avoid needing those features or any external service.

846
01:04:24.462 --> 01:04:29.338
The diagrams in this deck are original schematics for orientation.

847
01:04:29.338 --> 01:04:37.138
They are not captured product screenshots, and the video is narrated instruction rather than a live-session

848
01:04:37.138 --> 01:04:37.625
recording.

849
01:04:37.625 --> 01:04:45.425
The PowerPoint uses the visual language of the supplied reference deck without carrying forward its old

850
01:04:45.425 --> 01:04:47.862
teaching content or presenter affiliation.

851
01:04:47.862 --> 01:04:54.688
Use the transcript and captions if you prefer reading or need an accessible alternative.

852
